<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">attack</ansi>

Start a fight with a group of enemies. Once it starts, the battle plays out
on its own, the way your company was set up beforehand: nothing you type
changes it but a new company focus (<ansi fg="command">help tactics</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">attack [group]</ansi>
  Start a battle with a group, by the word the room shows for it:
  <ansi fg="command">attack ruffians</ansi> for "A band of ruffians (4)".

  <ansi fg="command">attack [group]#2</ansi>
  The second group of that name in the room ("a second band of ruffians").

  <ansi fg="command">attack [name]</ansi>
  A creature alone is attacked by its own name. So is another player,
  where the rules allow it.

You can't pick out one member of a group: the whole group fights together,
and you're told what to type ("The ruffian fights with a band of ruffians.
Type attack ruffians."). Look a group over first with <ansi fg="command">look [group]</ansi> or
<ansi fg="command">scout [group]</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Who you strike</ansi>

You start on the member your strategy picks: by default the weakest you
can reach from your place in your company's formation. Each companion
starts on its own strategy's choice. When a target falls or can't be
reached, its fighter turns to the next one the same way, with companions
beside you or alone. See <ansi fg="command">help strategy</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Once the battle is under way</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">attack</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">cast</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">backstab</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">shoot</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">tackle</ansi>, and <ansi fg="command">disarm</ansi> are held
until it's over ("The battle is under way: it plays out as you set it up."),
and so are <ansi fg="command">break</ansi>, walking out, <ansi fg="command">formation</ansi> and <ansi fg="command">strategy</ansi> changes,
eating, drinking, <ansi fg="command">use</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">equip</ansi>, and <ansi fg="command">remove</ansi>. Use <ansi fg="command">retreat</ansi> or <ansi fg="command">flee</ansi> to leave it.
Spells are cast by strategy, on their own.

Spells, backstabs, and shots don't start fights: begin with <ansi fg="command">attack</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Hitting</ansi>

Your chance to hit is {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitEven" }}% against an even match, moving toward {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitMax" }}% or
{{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitMin" }}% by your Attack against your target's Evasion (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>) and your
Speed against theirs (<ansi fg="command">help speed</ansi>). Your chance of a critical
hit comes from your Smarts against theirs (<ansi fg="command">help smarts</ansi>). Your company's
chemistry adds to your chance to hit, and darkness takes from it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How hard a blow lands</ansi>

A blow that gets past the target's block, parry, or dodge is one of three
qualities, and your skill edge over the target decides which is likely:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Quality</ansi>    <ansi fg="yellow">damage</ansi>   <ansi fg="yellow">even match</ansi>   <ansi fg="yellow">full edge for you</ansi>   <ansi fg="yellow">full edge against</ansi>
  glancing    half     {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.GlanceEven" }}%         {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.GlanceLeast" }}%                  {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.GlanceFull" }}%
  solid       normal   55%         45%                  45%
  telling     {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.TellingFactor" }}x      {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.TellingEven" }}%         {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.TellingFull" }}%                  {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.TellingLeast" }}%

A glancing blow is a graze, about a tenth of a fighter's health. A telling
blow finds a gap and does the most. The quality is named at the end of the
line: <ansi fg="command">(glancing, 3 damage)</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">(telling, 12 damage)</ansi>; a solid blow says nothing.
A critical hit comes on top of the quality. A wind-up's heavy blow is
always solid (<ansi fg="command">help interrupts</ansi>). Spells scale by the skill edge in their
own way (<ansi fg="command">help spells</ansi>).

See also: <ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help scout</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help targeting</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help formation</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
